  "response": "Where an operator and a fabricator each lay the rupture of a pressure vessel at the other's door, the dispute is resolved not by the parties' assertions but by the physical record left in the fractured metal, which preserves the sequence of events in a form neither side can edit. The analyst's task is therefore to read the fracture surface, the service pressure history and the construction record as mutually constraining bodies of evidence. The operator attributed the rupture to a fabrication defect left in the weld root. The fabricator attributed the rupture to overpressurization during service operation.\n\nFailure analysts examine the fracture surface for beach marks that indicate progressive crack growth. Ductile dimples and shear lips on the fracture surface indicate a single overload separation. Concentric beach marks around the initiation site are themselves the product of a single overload rupture, and that cannot be squared with treating beach marking as the register of crack extension accumulated cycle by cycle, for one band pattern on one surface cannot carry both meanings. At finer scale, fatigue striations on the fracture face are resolved using a scanning electron microscope. Fatigue striations on a steel fracture surface are visible to the unaided eye, and these two accounts of their scale cannot both be true, since a feature brought out only by electron optics is not one the naked eye picks out across a ruptured shell.\n\nCode conformance in the vessel's manufacture notwithstanding, the fracture origin was located at the toe of the circumferential seam weld; the radiographic record of the root condition of that same seam confirms that the separation began at that location. The vessel was fabricated to ASME Boiler and Pressure Vessel Code Section VIII requirements. Fabrication radiographs of the seam weld documented incomplete penetration at the root.\n\nOnly after the condition of the weld root had been set down in the fabrication record did the inquiry turn to the vessel's service pressure history. Pressure-recorder data were used to reconstruct the number of pressurization cycles the vessel experienced, and that reconstruction led investigators to compare the recorded cycle count against the design cycle life of the vessel. Crack-growth-rate calculations run backwards from the measured striation spacing then fix the interval over which the flaw extended to critical dimensions, and the two timelines are laid side by side. That comparison against the design cycle life supplies the evidence for the tribunal's holding that the rupture resulted from progressive fatigue cracking initiating at a fabrication defect, a determination returned after the pressurization history had been reconstructed from the recorder traces.",
